Darth. wrote:
>[BIg snip about class differentiation]

I've often thought that the different Gods should be more individual.
As it is, they all behave the same as one another (with some very minor
differences between the alignments.)

I would suggest that some Gods be made easier to anger than others, or
easier to appease when angry. Some will happily help you out of
starvation, but are less forthcoming when it comes to curing poison or
sickness. Some will turn a blind eye to the slaughter of peacefuls,
others will zap you for even looking at that hobbit funny. Some will
reward your sacrifices with sepllbooks and blessings, others prefer to
dish out weapons and AC points.

Divine behaviour modification needn't only apply to the player's God
either: The crossaligned Gods in your game would have their quirks as
well: Some don't really care too much if you convert their altar to
chaotic, others will chase you down through level after level with
summoned nasties and priests. Some are happy to accept convertees from
rical faiths, while others are likely to leave you out on the cold. [1]

And that's just the tip of the iceberg, I'm sure there are many other
ways in which God behaviour could be differentiated. Even with the rich
pantheon we have now, there should be more than enough permutations to
give each deity a unique personality. While late-game players probably
don't interact with their deities enough for this to make a huge
difference, it will certainly 'flavour' the early/ mid game and force
players to adopt different playing styles for the various roles.

[1]Which reminds me: I think in Nethack you should be able to piss off
*all* the Gods so that none of them will have you, leaving you
"Godless" until you do something wonderful to appease one of them.[2]

[2] You should also be able to convert and serve Moloch, if you like.



